  "reference_protocol": "Both public file modes are compared with SQLite 3.53.4 using complete SQL statements. Output, return code, normalized diagnostics and persisted catalog/rowids/rows/integrity must agree. Actual schema declarations drive all collation metadata; no expected outputs or failing rows are filtered.",
  "implementation": "Retained sources cache actual declared column programs. An internal, source-unforgeable expression annotation distinguishes implicit declarations from explicit COLLATE so explicit operators keep priority; unary plus and CAST inherit declarations while ordinary scalar functions do not. The ordinary typed evaluator/comparator handles values. CHECK/STORED programs bind declarations, correlation frames preserve them, and ORDER BY uses effective metadata. Public rollback/WAL and cold page readers decline BINARY-only plans for actual non-BINARY schema programs before their comparisons. Source-free physical Core commands remain BINARY and new automatic/explicit non-BINARY index paths are unadmitted.",
  "audit_correction": "The old literal oldChecks-plus-newChecks audit was updated to match the actual map that binds declared metadata before CHECK validation. Its correction has a separate receipt with unchanged runtime source and executable hashes. The original failing audit remains archived; no source rebuild or redundant SQL rerun was needed.",
  "proof_scope": "Registered component contracts retain compiled declarations, preserve identity for BINARY declarations/canonical tables and select the explicit BINARY resolver over an implicit NOCASE declaration. The latter is a name-selection equation, not a new full character-comparison proof. The runtime unit group checks cached metadata, implicit/explicit priority, encoded reopening and rejection of changed declarations or user calls to the internal annotation. Existing Core write proof domains remain restricted to physical BINARY commands; no general SQL collation-equivalence theorem is claimed.",
  "feature_probe_scope": "The 88-case unittest and fresh-seed survey share named families and one generator. Their 14 failures are not a global compatibility percentage.",
